.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT_CC 3 "2026-09-20" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct_cc \- declare and create an ordered map ordered by a custom comparison procedure
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct_cc(hm_name, key_t, val_t, custom_comparison_proc);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Declares an ordered map handle with the name hm_name. It also declares the
companion type variables. Those variables carry key_t and val_t to every
later type-inferred macro. The macro then creates a map. custom_comparison_proc
orders the keys of that map, and not the default order for key_t.

The macro is cbmap_construct(3) plus a comparison procedure. It uses the
default allocator. cbmap_construct_mp(3) replaces the allocator instead, and
cbmap_construct_full(3) takes both. cbmap_construct_cc_scoped(3) is the same
macro with a cleanup attribute. That attribute destroys the map at the exit
of the scope.

A struct key_t needs this variant. Without a procedure, the map orders a key
type that it does not recognize by a raw comparison of the representation.
Such a comparison reads padding bytes. It also orders a pointer member by
its address.

cbmap_declare(3) and then cbmap_init_cc(3) is the two-step form. Use that
form when the caller must handle a creation failure and the failure must not
be fatal.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name                 Name for the map handle to declare
key_t                   Key type. It tells every later macro how to read
                        a key. It also tells the container which width to
                        expect and which alignment to give a stored key
val_t                   Value type. Only the type-inferred macros read it.
                        The library never gives it to the container
custom_comparison_proc  A ccol_comparison_proc_t, or NULL for the
                        default order for key_t
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
None. This macro declares variables and does not expand to an expression.
.fi
.SH "CUSTOM COMPARISON"
.nf
The procedure gets pointers to two keys. It returns a negative value when
the first key orders before the second key. It returns a positive value when
the first key orders after the second key. It returns zero when the map must
treat the two keys as the same key. A zero makes a call an update and not an
insert. The procedure therefore defines key equality and key order.

The procedure must give a strict weak order over key_t. That order must not
change while the map holds elements. The same pair of keys must always
compare in the same way. A comparison in the opposite direction must give
the opposite sign. The order must also be transitive. An inconsistent
procedure breaks the placement invariant of the tree. After that, the map
cannot find a key that it holds.

The procedure gets two bare pointers and no lengths. It can only read a
width that key_t fixes. The map therefore validates the width of an
incoming key against key_t. The map also aligns its stored keys for key_t.
The procedure decides the order of the keys and nothing else.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program through ccol_fatal_err() if the creation fails
The macro uses the default allocator
The procedure must stay callable for the whole life of the map
The walk visits the elements in ascending key order, by the order of this
procedure. cbmap_begin_iter(3) describes that contract
Destroy the map with cbmap_destroy(3). The companion type variables follow
the scope of the handle. A function that receives the handle therefore needs
cbmap_redeclare(3) there
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
typedef struct { int major; int minor; } version;

int version_cmp(const void *a, const void *b) {
    version x, y;
    memcpy(&x, a, sizeof(x));   /* a key can be at any address */
    memcpy(&y, b, sizeof(y));
    if (x.major != y.major) {
        return (x.major > y.major) - (x.major < y.major);
    }
    return (x.minor > y.minor) - (x.minor < y.minor);
}

cbmap_construct_cc(releases, version, char *, version_cmp);
cbmap_destroy(releases);
.fi
